# Content text is canonical JSONC compared byte for byte (07 §1.7): LF on Windows checkouts too. Listed
# per extension so binary sources keep git's binary detection.
*.hrec text eol=lf
*.hcont text eol=lf
*.hent text eol=lf
*.meta text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.jsonc text eol=lf

# Binary sources are Git LFS objects (07 §1.7), `lockable` so that editing one asks for its LFS lock. Only
# the content types helios-cook check knows; it refuses a tracked one that git stores outside LFS, and an
# extension in upper case (these patterns are case-sensitive on Linux). CI checkouts keep the pointers
# (actions/checkout's default `lfs: false`).
*.glb filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text lockable
*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text lockable
*.exr filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text lockable
